From 20236deb874279b0db68a56d01a40094ea15b7ab Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ObeseCatLord <6099645+ObeseCatLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 10:12:46 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] iop: FIFO ready queues with priority preemption; kernel
 semaphore results (H-IOP5, H-IOP14)

Scheduler (H-IOP5): equal priorities used to run lowest-id-first with a
re-pick after every slice, and a readied higher-priority thread waited for
the 256-instruction slice to end.
- every ready thread has a place in its priority's FIFO queue; a slice that
  merely ends keeps the thread at the front (the IOP kernel does not time
  slice), RotateThreadReadyQueue(prio) rotates the named queue, and
  ChangeThreadPriority moves a ready thread to the back of its new queue
- anything that readies a thread (StartThread, WakeupThread, ResumeThread,
  SignalSema, SetEventFlag, DeleteSema/DeleteEventFlag, ReleaseWait, RPC
  completion, expired DelayThread) preempts the running thread immediately
  when it outranks it; the preempted thread keeps its place in its queue
- DisableDispatchThread/EnableDispatchThread are honoured (preemption is
  deferred and applied on enable); expired delays become ready in wake order
- semaphore waiters are served by (priority, arrival) instead of thread id

Kernel results (H-IOP14):
- SignalSema returns KE_SEMA_OVF instead of silently saturating
- DeleteSema releases waiters with KE_WAIT_DELETE (they used to wait forever)
- WaitSema that would block outside a scheduled thread (callback, module
  start routine, driver callback) returns KE_CAN_NOT_WAIT with v0 written and
  one logged warning; it no longer parks the interrupted thread on the
  callback's behalf or leaves v0 unwritten
- a thread whose last instruction tail-calls a blocking import keeps its wait
  instead of being retired at the return sentinel

Tests: new ps2_iop_kernel_sched_tests (12 cases).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
